+
+## JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+const config = { servers: [{ host: "a.example" }], debug: true, log: { level: "info" } };
+
+applyMergePatch(config, { debug: null, log: { level: "warn" }, servers: [{ host: "b.example" }] });
+// { servers: [{ host: "b.example" }], log: { level: "warn" } }
+```
+
+- `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` merges `patch` into `target` and returns the result as a new
+  value. Neither input is modified, and the result shares no value with them.
+- A patch object changes the target member by member: a `null` member removes the member (or does
+  nothing if it is absent), any other value is merged into it the same way, recursively. A target that
+  is not an object is first replaced by `{}`.
+- Any other patch, an array or a string, number, boolean or `null`, replaces the target whole. Arrays
+  are never merged element by element, so a merge patch cannot change one array element, nor set a
+  member to `null`; use JSON Patch for that.
+- It throws an `InvalidMergePatchError` when `patch` is not a JSON value: it is or holds `undefined`,
+  a non-finite number, a function, a class instance, a sparse array, or a value that contains itself.
+  No target makes a valid merge patch fail.
+
+See [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-json-merge-patch.md) for the choices made where the RFC leaves room.
